  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAs of 750 AM Monday...\n\nThe flash flood watch for portions of the region for this\nafternoon into the this evening has been cancelled. While soils\nremain at least partially compromised from recent rainfall,\nguidance does not support rainfall amounts sufficient for\nrenewed flash flooding with ensemble threshold probabilities \nfor more than 1 inch in 6 hours less than 10%.\n\nMainly minor water issues will continue across portions of SE OH\nas water continues to work down the lazy drainages.\n\nAs of 217 AM Monday...\n\nA cold front will drift slowly southeastward today before\nstalling. Models show that thunderstorms are again possible this\nafternoon and evening over eastern West Virginia and\nsouthwestern Virginia. HRRR is especially concerning, showing\nsome areas of 2 to 4 inches of rainfall this afternoon.\nTherefore, have issued a Flood Watch for flash flooding for\neastern West Virginia and southwestern Virginia for this\nafternoon and evening. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA cold front will continue to waffle near the area with waves \nmoving along that front for Tuesday and Wednesday. This will \nkeep showers, and the chance for mainly afternoon and evening \nthunderstorms. With the ground already saturated in many areas,\nthere will continue to be a flash flood threat for portions of\nthe area both days, but less likely in southeast Ohio and\nnorthwestern West Virginia on Tuesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA cold front will push south of the area Thursday and Friday, \nbringing a break from the precipitation for much of the region.\nSome models show that there could be enough moisture over \nsouthwest Virginia and extreme southern West Virginia to see \nsome showers and thunderstorms, but other models indicate dry\nweather should rule.",
